      "question": "What are the ACID properties in database transactions?",
      "answer": "ACID stands for Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability. Atomicity guarantees all operations in a transaction succeed completely or roll back entirely (all-or-nothing). Consistency ensures the database transitions between valid states, preserving all schema constraints and foreign keys. Isolation ensures concurrently executing transactions do not interfere with one another, avoiding dirty or non-repeatable reads. Durability guarantees that once committed, changes permanently survive system crashes or power failures via write-ahead logging.",
