      "question": "How does a hash table resolve collisions?",
      "answer": "Hash collisions occur when distinct keys hash to the same bucket index. The two primary resolution techniques are Separate Chaining and Open Addressing. In separate chaining, each table index points to a linked list or self-balancing binary search tree holding colliding items. In open addressing, all entries occupy the main table array directly; when a collision occurs, alternative vacant slots are located using probing sequences such as linear probing, quadratic probing, or double hashing.",
