      "question": "Why do we use caching in distributed systems?",
      "answer": "In distributed systems, caching dramatically reduces read latencies and protects persistent storage databases from query saturation by exploiting temporal and spatial locality. High-speed in-memory caches like Redis or Memcached serve hot data in sub-millisecond time. Standard caching patterns include Cache-Aside, Write-Through, and Write-Back, coupled with TTLs and eviction policies like LRU or LFU to manage memory bounds and mitigate stale reads.",
